First you need to edit your .bashrc to set the variable SOFTHOME to
the directory above the one you are going to unzip the software into.
I use:

export SOFTHOME=~/software

which is a directory you need to have already created. You also need
to add $SOFTHOME/bin/linux to your path using:

export PATH=$PATH:$SOFTHOME/bin/linux

Then you need to unzip the code into the appropriate directory by
moving into $SOFTHOME and typing:

tar jxfv nrs.tbz2; mv nrs-x.y nrs

The second command is necessary, as the version number is appended to
the directory name in storage just for reference, and should not be
kept.

or by downloading from the CVS source on taxis.stir.ac.uk if you have
a login by typing the following from your home directory:

export CVS_RSH=ssh
export CVSROOT=:ext:<username>@taxis.stir.ac.uk/var/cvsroot
cvs co software/nrs

Then go into the nrs directory and type:

make install

to create all the necessary directories and build the executables.

Documentation will now be in $SOFTHOME/doc, linux executables in
$SOFTHOME/bin/linux and linux kernel modules in
$SOFTHOME/lib/modules/linux. Koala and Khepera executables are in
$SOFTHOME/bin/koala and khepera respectively, but are downloaded
automatically, so you don't need to worry about that.

See general.ps/pdf for more general information, and script.ps/pdf and
model.ps/pdf for documentation on the scripting language and model.

For subsequent installs it is sufficient to move to the nrs directory
and type:

cvs update

if you have cvs access, or to follow the instructions for
uncompressing again if not.

To copy a modified installation elsewhere, simply type:

make bzip2

This will remove all of the unnecessary files (object files,
executables and backups), and then zip up the rest into $SOFTHOME.
